    "message": "feat(analytics): split token columns per provider and report prompt caching\n\nTwo gaps in the model request analytics.\n\nPer-provider and per-key rows collapsed input and output into a single \"Tokens\"\nnumber, even though the store already returned them separately -- the model\ntable showed \"in / out\" while these did not, so the same data was presented two\ndifferent ways and per-provider usage could not actually be read off the page.\nBoth tables now report input and output separately.\n\nNothing tracked prompt caching at all, which for cache-heavy workloads is most\nof the input bill. Usage already carried cache_creation_input_tokens and\ncache_read_input_tokens (DeepSeek populates them), and the streaming capture\nread input_tokens out of exactly the dict those sit in, but discarded them.\n\nThey are now captured, stored in two new columns, aggregated into totals and\ninto the provider/key breakdowns, and surfaced as Input (uncached), Cached\ninput, Cache hit rate and Cache writes. tokens_in deliberately remains the\n*uncached* portion, matching Anthropic's semantics, so total prompt input is\nuncached + reads + writes; the hit rate divides reads by that total rather than\nby tokens_in alone, which would otherwise read well above 100%.\n\nStorage follows the existing post-release pattern: columns arrive through\n_ADDED_COLUMNS so databases created by earlier versions migrate in place. The\ncovering index gains both columns and is renamed to v3 -- CREATE INDEX IF NOT\nEXISTS would otherwise keep the v2 index and silently drop index-only coverage,\nthe exact trap its own comment documents.\n\nVerified against a copy of a real request log: existing rows survive the\nmigration, a recorded request reports 900 cached against 100 uncached tokens,\nand the provider breakdown yields a 90.0% hit rate. The helper renders 0.0% for\nuncached traffic and an em dash when there is nothing to divide.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
